 <section class="opening" id="primitive"><div class="eyebrow chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 01</strong></div><div><h2>Primitive discovery</h2><p>This is a collection of research questions driven by a different perspective on quantum computing.</p><p>Quantum computing comes from everywhere. Many of its ideas are found and remixed across fields; here I want to follow the connections between angular momentum, exchange symmetry, and computation.</p><p>I think of this as <strong>primitive discovery.</strong> Consider the quantum Fourier transform, quantum walks, and Regev’s reduction. I think the qualitative descriptions of these objects are as important as the quantitative ones; they are inextricably linked.</p><p>I emphasize the primitive of measuring the total spin of a chosen subset of qubits. This can be understood through the lenses of angular momentum, generalized SWAP tests, and representation theory. I think this perspective could help us explain quantum speedups and identify new algorithms.</p><p>The questions are a way to test this perspective. Does it give a clearer account of an existing algorithm, suggest a new one, or expose a constraint that becomes a lower bound? A useful description should help us formulate and prove quantitative statements.</p><aside class="editorial-note" aria-label="A note on this experimental site"><p>This site is experimental. I used a lot of AI assistance to bring these ideas to life. This is still uncomfortable for me. But I think this perspective is important and fascinating enough to share now, with a number of questions that we can think about together.</p></aside></div></section>
 <UniversalityStory/>
 <SwapStory/>
 <section class="essay-section" id="languages"><aside class="section-aside chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 03</strong></aside><div class="prose"><h2>Spin and representation theory</h2><p>For a subsystem <Math tex="A"/> of qubits, define</p><Math display tex={String.raw`\mathbf J_A=\frac12\sum_{i\in A}(X_i,Y_i,Z_i),\qquad \mathbf J_A^2=J_{A,x}^2+J_{A,y}^2+J_{A,z}^2.`}/><p>Measuring <Math tex={String.raw`\mathbf J_A^2`}/> returns an eigenvalue <Math tex="j(j+1)"/>. The pair measurement above is the case <Math tex={String.raw`|A|=2`}/>. For larger subsystems, a spin value labels a whole subspace, including any repeated copies of the same spin representation.</p><p>To describe the computation, we need to know which degrees of freedom these measurements preserve and which they change. Collective rotations and permutations provide the decomposition.</p></div></section>
 <div class="wide-section"><div class="dictionary">
  <article><span class="eyebrow">Angular momentum</span><h3>Total spin</h3><p>Two spin-½ particles have total spin <Math tex="J=0"/> or <Math tex="J=1"/>. The singlet has <Math tex="J=0"/>; the three-dimensional triplet space has <Math tex="J=1"/>. With <Math tex={"\\hbar=1"}/>, <Math tex={"\\mathbf J^2"}/> has eigenvalue <Math tex="J(J+1)"/>.</p><Math display tex={"|s\\rangle=\\frac{|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}"}/></article>
  <article><span class="eyebrow">Measurement</span><h3>SWAP eigenvalue</h3><p>An antisymmetric state changes sign under SWAP; a symmetric state is unchanged. The singlet has eigenvalue −1 and every triplet has eigenvalue +1. Measuring this eigenvalue projects onto one of these spaces while preserving coherence within it.</p><Math display tex={"\\begin{aligned}P_s&=\\frac{I-\\mathrm{SWAP}}2\\\\P_t&=\\frac{I+\\mathrm{SWAP}}2\\end{aligned}"}/></article>
  <article><span class="eyebrow">Representation theory</span><h3>SU(2) decomposition</h3><p>Under a collective rotation <Math tex={"U\\otimes U"}/>, the singlet and triplet spaces are irreducible representations of dimensions 1 and 3. For three qubits, spin ½ occurs twice: an additional label distinguishes the two copies.</p><Math display tex={"\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\cong V_0\\oplus V_1"}/></article>
 </div></div>
 <RepresentationStory/>
 <div class="wide-section"><SchurLab/></div>
 <section class="essay-section" id="resource"><aside class="section-aside chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 04</strong></aside><div class="prose"><h2>Measuring overlapping pairs</h2><p>Prepare two singlets, then measure an overlapping pair. In one branch, the singlets reappear on new pairings. This is what I mean when I say that the singlet, or the antisymmetry, “jumps around.” In the other branch, the state has a different pattern of pairwise correlations. Nothing physically travels along the lines in the figure; they describe the probabilities of subsequent singlet outcomes.</p><p>Try measuring 1–3, then 1–2. Repeating the same measurement is certain. Changing the pair can make the outcome uncertain again. These overlapping subsystem observables do not all commute.</p></div></section>
 <div class="wide-section"><SpinLab/><QuestionLink id="Q34"/></div>
 <section class="essay-section essay-continuation"><div class="prose"><h2>Antisymmetry as a resource?</h2><p>Is the resource antisymmetry itself, or the information encoded in how antisymmetry is distributed? These are different proposals. For fixed global spin, the sum of all pairwise singlet probabilities is fixed, but their distribution can change. States can also differ in multiplicity-space coherence that this sum does not record. A candidate resource should distinguish states according to a specified computational task, rather than simply count singlet weight.</p><p>The fully symmetric sector provides a useful comparison: every internal pair is triplet, so internal STP leaves its state unchanged. Extra qubits can change the available operations. A resource theory needs a task, a set of free states, and a restricted set of free operations. Calling the full universal STP model free would make many interesting states cheap by definition. We have to say which restriction makes the proposed resource costly. <QuestionLink id="Q10"/></p><h3>States within a spin sector</h3><p>STP preserves global spin sector: its projectors commute with collective rotations. Conditioning can change the weights assigned to different sectors of a mixed input, while transforming the state inside their multiplicity spaces. It does not freely create coherence between distinct global-spin sectors.</p><p>For maximally mixed input, sector J has probability (2J + 1) dim M_J / 2ⁿ; typical total spin grows on the order of √n, rather than being concentrated at zero. The interesting question is what conditional protocols can organize inside these sectors.</p><p>Even within J = 0, there is more than one state. Here the total amount of pairwise singlet weight is fixed. Change a relative phase and watch that same total redistribute.</p></div></section>
 <div class="wide-section"><CoherenceLab/><div class="two-questions"><QuestionLink id="Q11"/><QuestionLink id="Q12"/></div></div>
 <PreparationStory/>
 <section class="essay-section" id="preparation"><aside class="section-aside chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 05</strong></aside><div class="prose"><h2>Preparation and verification</h2><p>Quantum Max-Cut asks for a state with a large positive weighted sum of singlet-projector expectations. Given copies of a candidate state, local tests can estimate that objective. Finding or preparing a good state is a different problem. This gap between verification and preparation feels central to the perspective. <Cite keys={['cubitt-montanaro-classification','piddock-montanaro-antiferromagnetic']}/></p><p>It also brings us to product tests and unentangled witnesses. SWAP measurements compare quantum systems; product testing relates their outcomes to subsystem purities. The QMA(2) model assumes unentanglement between witnesses; the test’s soundness analysis uses that independence assumption. <Cite keys="harrow-montanaro-product-test"/></p><QuestionLink id="Q15"/><QuestionLink id="Q38"/><QuestionLink id="Q30"/><h3>Quantum spin-SAT</h3><p>Each constraint specifies a subsystem and a total-spin value that it is forbidden to have. If <Math tex={"\\Pi_{S,j}"}/> projects subsystem <Math tex="S"/> onto spin <Math tex="j"/>, the constraint requires <Math tex={"\\Pi_{S,j}|\\psi\\rangle=0"}/>. Can one state satisfy all of these conditions on overlapping subsystems? What changes if the witness must be a product state?</p><Math display tex={"H=\\sum_a\\Pi_{S_a,j_a},\\qquad H|\\psi\\rangle=0."}/><p>Fixing the constraint size and the promise gap gives a concrete complexity question. Forcing a spin value is a different clause, with penalty <Math tex={"I-\\Pi_{S,j}"}/>.</p><QuestionLink id="Q35"/><h3>The EPR interaction and its neighbors</h3><p>In work with James Sud, I study a complexity phase transition at the EPR Hamiltonian. Move a single local energy level and the associated many-body problem passes through sharply different complexity regimes. This gives a precise setting in which to ask how the singlet’s position affects complexity. <Cite keys="marwaha-sud-phase-transition"/></p></div></section>
 <div class="wide-section"><PhaseLab/><div class="two-questions"><QuestionLink id="Q13"/><QuestionLink id="Q14"/></div></div>
 <section class="essay-section essay-continuation" id="classical-guides"><div class="prose"><h2>Start from a cut of the same graph</h2><p>The EPR algorithm begins with a fully polarized state, which is easy to prepare. Why not try a similar approach on the hard side of the phase diagram, starting from a Max-Cut solution? Finding the cut may be NP-hard, but its bit string has an efficient description. Merlin could supply it; Arthur could prepare that product state and try to move from there.</p><p>The graph should stay the same. Write the Quantum Max-Cut energy as <Math tex={"Q_G=-\\sum_{ij}w_{ij}P_s^{ij}"}/>. A computational-basis state <Math tex={"|x\\rangle"}/> has energy <Math tex={"\\langle x|Q_G|x\\rangle=-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Cut}_G(x)"}/>. A maximum cut therefore gives a best computational-basis starting state for this objective. It does not supply a quantum ground state.</p><p>Here is a concrete interpolation to try. Let <Math tex="P_{t_0}"/> project onto <Math tex={"(|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2"}/>, one particular triplet, and put</p><Math display tex={"H_G(t)=-\\sum_{ij}w_{ij}\\bigl(P_s^{ij}+(1+t)P_{t_0}^{ij}\\bigr),\\quad 0\\le t\\le1."}/><p>At <Math tex="t=0"/>, this is the classical Max-Cut Hamiltonian <Math tex={"-\\sum_{ij}w_{ij}(I-Z_iZ_j)/2"}/>. At <Math tex="t=1"/>, its local levels are −2 for <Math tex="t_0"/>, −1 for the singlet, and 0 for the other two triplets. This is twice the <Math tex="s=1/2"/> interaction in Experiment 4, whose ground-energy problem is StoqMA-complete. <Cite keys="marwaha-sud-phase-transition"/></p><figure class="witness-route"><figcaption class="eyebrow">A candidate verification procedure</figcaption><ol><li><Math tex="x"/><span>A cut on G</span><small>Merlin supplies a bit string, a sector, and any path parameters.</small></li><li><Math tex="H_G(t)"/><span>Quantum interpolation</span><small>Prepare the product state and follow an efficiently implementable path.</small></li><li><Math tex={"E\\le a"}/><span>Energy verification</span><small>Arthur measures the target energy against the promised thresholds.</small></li></ol></figure><p>This path is part of a direct interpolation from Quantum Max-Cut: hold the singlet coefficient fixed and lower the selected triplet from 0 to −2. The classical Max-Cut point lies halfway along that route. Starting there gives a classically described product state; starting at a Quantum Max-Cut ground state would require explaining how that state was prepared.</p><p>There is an immediate obstruction: this path preserves Hamming weight. A maximum cut can be in a different magnetization sector from the target ground state. A cut-aligned Z field can select a starting state, but cannot move it between these sectors. Perhaps the witness should instead specify a good cut in a useful sector. We then need a polynomial gap along the path, or another preparation argument giving enough low-energy overlap.</p><p>This is the point of the proposed containment <Math tex={"\\mathrm{StoqMA}\\subseteq\\mathrm{QCMA}"}/>: the space might be navigable from a good starting position, even if finding that position is hard. The claim needs a short useful guide for <em>every</em> YES instance. The verifier need not prove that the supplied cut is optimal; it must reach low energy when a good guide exists, and reject all guides on NO instances.</p><p>The Lee–Yang gap theorem proves this kind of quantum preparation bound for its stated interaction class and uniform field. It does not yet justify cut-aligned fields here. A classical Markov-chain version would separately need a mixing bound and efficiently computable transitions. These Hamiltonian paths also permit operations beyond bare STP: the selected triplet distinguishes a spatial axis. <Cite keys={['rayudu-takahashi-lee-yang','wong-bravyi-gosset-liu-lee-yang','weggemans-folkertsma-cade-guidable','bravyi-guided-stoquastic']}/></p><QuestionLink id="Q19"/><p>A broader question is: which spin-constraint problems have classical guides as witnesses, and which are QCMA-complete? That question remains useful independently of the proposed containment.</p><QuestionLink id="Q40"/></div></section>
 <section class="essay-section" id="duality"><aside class="section-aside chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 06</strong></aside><div class="prose"><h2>Computational forms of duality</h2><p>A Fourier transform changes the description in which a computation is simple. Schur–Weyl duality suggests asking a similar question: what becomes easy when we describe a state by total spin and multiplicity instead of individual qubits? Could the two group actions support complementary ways to compute?</p><p>The Schur transform expresses the state in coordinates <Math tex={"|J,m,\\alpha\\rangle"}/>: total spin, orientation, and multiplicity. Collective rotations act on <Math tex="m"/> and permutations act on <Math tex={"\\alpha"}/>. This resembles Fourier analysis because it resolves group actions into irreducible pieces. It is not literally a swap between equal-sized registers. A computational duality would say which operations or information can be obtained efficiently from access to the other description.</p><p>Sampling an irrep label, changing coupling-tree coordinates coherently, and extracting a representation while preserving entanglement with a reference are different tasks. Weak permutational computation already has a polynomial-time STP simulation; the coherent tasks need their own resource analysis. <Cite keys="freedman-hastings-shokrian-zini"/></p><p>Random SWAP tests can perform certain qubit purification and Schur-sampling tasks. I find this interesting partly because an algorithm may need only a particular piece of the information supplied by a Schur transform. <Cite keys="brahmachari-hulse-pfister-marvian"/></p><p>There is also an established computational duality relating group representations and coherent Fourier subspace extraction, with applications to quantum money. Its assumptions and preservation of quantum information inside the extracted spaces matter. Can this help us identify a useful task expressed in subsystem-spin measurements? <Cite keys="bostanci-nehoran-zhandry-duality"/></p><QuestionLink id="Q20"/><QuestionLink id="Q05"/></div></section><section class="essay-section essay-continuation"><div class="prose"><h2>Beyond pairwise qubit measurements</h2><p>The primitive can also be varied. A three- or four-qubit total-spin measurement resolves different subspaces from a sequence of pair tests. Even when the unrestricted computational power is the same, depth, adaptivity, and the retained state may differ.</p><p>For qudits, we can measure irreducible labels of SU(d). More generally, fix a representation of a compact group and ask what computation is generated by measuring the irrep of a small subsystem. Which representations and measurements support universality? Which restrictions admit efficient classical simulation?</p><QuestionLink id="Q24"/><QuestionLink id="Q39"/></div></section>
 <section class="essay-section essay-continuation" id="shallow"><div class="prose"><h2>What is a shallow spin computation?</h2><p>If STP is our primitive, I want analogues of familiar circuit-depth questions: what can constant depth do, and what changes at logarithmic depth? We have to count the classical computation between measurements as well. An unrestricted classical controller could compute Majority itself.</p><p>A concrete benchmark is to encode each unknown input bit in a pair: 1 as a singlet, 0 as the mixed triplet sector. For an odd number of pairs, can a shallow STP protocol determine whether most pairs are singlets? One parallel measurement layer reads all the bits; logarithmic-depth classical aggregation gives a baseline. The question is whether quantum processing helps when the <em>total</em> classical-control depth is restricted, for example to AC⁰.</p><p>A second benchmark is intrinsically about spin: distinguish a state supported on low total-spin sectors from one supported on high total-spin sectors. Specify the separation between the sectors, the number of copies, and whether the state must be retained. Total spin is not the Hamming weight of a bit string, so this is a different threshold problem.</p><QuestionLink id="Q06"/><QuestionLink id="Q37"/></div></section>
 <section class="essay-section" id="adversary"><aside class="section-aside chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 07</strong></aside><div class="prose"><h2>Purification and adversary arguments</h2><p>There is another use of purification that belongs in this story. An algorithm receives an unknown oracle. In the analysis, we can keep the hidden instance coherently in a private register F. Tracing out F recovers the same distribution of instances that the algorithm sees.</p><p>The algorithm cannot touch F. Between queries, operations on its own workspace A leave the reduced state of F unchanged. Oracle queries can change it. Representation theory can organize that hidden record and suggest an observable that measures progress.</p></div></section>
 <div class="wide-section"><div class="register-diagram"><div class="eyebrow">A proof register, not an extra algorithmic resource</div><div class="register-row"><div>F / Hidden instance<small>Organize by symmetry.<br/>Track information gained.</small></div><Math display tex={String.raw`|\Psi_t\rangle=\sum_f\sqrt{p_f}\,|f\rangle_F|\psi_f^t\rangle_A`}/></div><div class="register-row"><div>A / Algorithm<small>Query the oracle.<br/>Operate on the workspace.</small></div><Math display tex={String.raw`\rho_A^t=\mathrm{Tr}_F|\Psi_t\rangle\langle\Psi_t|`}/></div><p class="register-caption">A lower bound compares initial progress, the progress required for success, and how much one query can change it.</p></div></div>
 <section class="essay-section essay-continuation"><div class="prose"><h2>Related lower-bound methods</h2><p>This is an existing family of ideas: symmetry-assisted adversaries, compressed permutation oracles, and group path recording. Recent work explicitly connects compressed-oracle arguments to multiplicative adversaries. I would like to understand whether subsystem spin suggests a useful formulation or application. <Cite keys={['ambainis-magnin-roetteler-roland','rosmanis-permutation','foxman-lombardi-ma-nehoran-wright','jeffery-zur-compressed-adversary']}/></p><p>We should keep the full representation structure in view. Sector probabilities alone may forget exactly the multiplicity and coherence information that a sharp argument needs. And this mathematical purification trick is distinct from physically purifying noisy qubits.</p><QuestionLink id="Q17"/><p>Symmetric and antisymmetric subspaces also appear in a recent quantum-oracle separation between QMA(2) and QMA and in disentangler lower bounds. That is a connection worth pursuing, with the oracle model and dimension conventions intact. <Cite keys={['bostanci-et-al-qma2-oracle','jeronimo-wu-xu-de-finetti']}/></p><QuestionLink id="Q18"/></div></section>
 <section class="closing" id="agenda"><div class="eyebrow chapter-heading"><strong class="chapter-number">Section 08</strong></div><h2>Questions I would like to understand</h2><p>I want to write these questions down so that people or agents can try to work on them. As more of research becomes automated, I think asking good questions may become even more important than finding answers. Deciding what is worth understanding, and finding a useful way to ask about it, is itself research. See also Kostis Kiriakakis’s <a href="https://kiriakakis.net/comics/mused/a-day-at-the-park"><em>A Day at the Park</em></a>.</p><p>The questions below concern algorithms, restricted models, resources, verification, duality, complexity, and physical realizations. I have tried to state the assumptions and suggest a place to start. Some formulations will surely need revision.</p><p>The stronger hypothesis is that several familiar quantum primitives admit a common description through the creation, transport, measurement, and delocalization of subsystem symmetry. Spin is the simplest case. I want to know when this description gives a simpler algorithm, a sharper lower bound, or a clearer account of quantum verification.</p><QuestionLink id="Q29"/><a class="text-link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}questions/`}>Read the {questions.length} research questions <span>↗</span></a></section>
</Layout>
